# tools/commit_message_guard_witness.rish -- the commit-message wall stands, and answers on both sides.
#
# Two commit-message rules in .claude/rules/git-signing.md were habits rather than walls, and a
# renderer can break a habit quietly. GitHub linkifies `#<number>` into the issue or pull request
# of that number, and `@word` into the user or org of that name -- backticks and good intentions
# stop neither. The cost was measured before this guard existed: 230 of our own citations across
# 249 commit messages had been rendering as links to unrelated equinox pull requests, cleared by
# the debride of `20260820.005542`.
#
# The guard is tools/hooks/commit-msg, armed by pointing core.hooksPath at the tree's own tracked
# hook directory (tools/install_hooks.rish), so the wall travels with the tree rather than living
# on one machine's untracked .git/hooks.
#
# This witness proves three things, and the second is the one a careless guard fails:
#   1. The refusals refuse -- our numbering with a hash, and a bare mention, each caught.
#   2. The welcomes welcome -- `PR #76`, `issue #12`, Urbit's own `Resolves #34.` that
#      .claude/rules/commit-messages.md endorses, an email's `@`, git's own comment lines, and a
#      verbose diff below the scissors. A wall that refuses everything is as useless as none.
#   3. The wall is actually armed on this clone, and the refusal teaches the fix rather than
#      merely saying no.
#   4. The public seed arms the same wall. publish-seed.sh re-creates seed/.git on every run,
#      so the arming lives in the script -- checked by doing it, not by reading it.
#
# Kin: tools/fixtures/commit_message_guard_scan.sh (the cases), tools/hooks/commit-msg (the wall),
# tools/install_hooks.rish (arming it), .claude/rules/git-signing.md (both rules).
#
# This authors no commit -- it feeds planted messages to the shipped hook and compares.
# Purely local: no key, no signature, no network, no funds, no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/commit_message_guard_witness.rish

say "commit-guard: Language EN -- the commit-message wall stands, and answers on both sides."
say "commit-guard: Style Radiant -- a guard is worth what its welcomes are worth; the cases that must pass are checked as hard as the cases that must fail."
say "commit-guard: Lens TAME -- the shipped hook is fed planted messages, never a copy, so this can never pass by measuring something the tree does not run. Purely local -- no key, no network, no funds, no device."

let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/commit_message_guard_scan.sh"]
assert scan.ok else "commit-guard: the guard scan failed to run -- ${scan.err}"
say scan.out

assert scan.out contains "GUARD_HOOK_PRESENT 1" else "commit-guard: tools/hooks/commit-msg is missing or not executable -- git would skip it in silence"

# Every planted case answered the way the rules say it should.
assert scan.out contains "GUARD_FAIL 0" else "commit-guard: a planted case answered the wrong way -- read the GUARD_CASE_BAD line above for which"
assert scan.out contains "GUARD_PASS 20" else "commit-guard: the case roster changed size -- 20 cases are expected, so add the new one here when you add it there"

# A refusal that does not name the fix costs the author a trip to the rules.
assert scan.out contains "GUARD_TEACHES 1" else "commit-guard: the refusal does not name the percent form and the rule it comes from"

# A guard must never eat the work it refuses.
assert scan.out contains "GUARD_MESSAGE_KEPT 1" else "commit-guard: the hook altered the message file it refused -- a refusal must leave the author's words untouched"

# The public seed arms the same wall. publish-seed.sh deletes and re-creates seed/.git on
# every publish, so a hooksPath set once by hand would be wiped on the next run -- the arming
# has to live in the script, and it is proven by doing rather than by reading: a throwaway
# repo armed exactly the way the publisher arms the seed refuses a forbidden message and
# welcomes a clean one, and the message the publisher actually ships is fed to the hook.
assert scan.out contains "SEED_PUBLISHER_ARMS 1" else "commit-guard: publish-seed.sh no longer arms the seed repo -- a fresh seed/.git would publish unguarded"
assert scan.out contains "SEED_ARMED_REFUSES 1" else "commit-guard: a repo armed the way the publisher arms the seed let a forbidden message through"
assert scan.out contains "SEED_ARMED_WELCOMES 1" else "commit-guard: a repo armed the seed's way refused a clean message -- the wall would block every publish"
assert scan.out contains "SEED_SHIPPED_MESSAGE_PASSES 1" else "commit-guard: the message publish-seed.sh actually ships does not pass the wall it arms"

# The wall must be standing here, not merely shippable. A fresh clone lands RED with a one-line fix.
assert scan.out contains "GUARD_ARMED 1" else "commit-guard: this clone is not armed -- run `rishi/bin/rishi run tools/install_hooks.rish` to point core.hooksPath at tools/hooks"

assert scan.out contains "GUARD_OK" else "commit-guard: the scan reported GUARD_BAD -- read the line above"
assert ((scan.out contains "GUARD_BAD") == false) else "commit-guard: GUARD_BAD present"

say "GREEN: commit-guard -- 20 planted cases answered right, the refusal teaches its own fix, the message survives it, the public seed arms the same wall on every publish, and this clone stands armed."
